  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1159 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nA band of light snow continues to push east this evening, \nthough low level dry air is keeping it very light, with various \ncameras showing very little to none sticking to roads. However, \nit looked like there were some wet spots out there. As \ntemperatures continue to fall below freezing tonight, that \nmoisture may freeze and lead to a few slick spots into Wednesday\nmorning, though with general lack of accumulation, impacts will\nlikely be pretty isolated.\n\nMost of the snow should come to an end shortly after sunrise, \nbut with the responsible shortwave trough/500 mb low being right\nover the forecast area on Wednesday, could see a few additional\nspotty flurries/sprinkles/showers develop, though chances are \nbelow 10%. Otherwise, expect another cool day with highs in the \nupper 30s to lower 40s.\n\nFor Thanksgiving, the low will be pushing into the Ohio River \nValley with a decent band of snow coming to an end/switching to \nrain along the MO/IL border, so heads up if you're traveling \nthat direction. Closer to home, surface ridging will be in \ncontrol and should lead to a quiet, cool day, with highs in the \nlower to mid 40s and a decent amount of sunshine for most.\n\nWe'll see a brief period of upper level ridging on Friday with \nsoutherly low level flow allowing us to warm back up into the \nmid 40s to lower 50s. Some of those warmer temperatures could \nstick around on Saturday, especially in the southern half of the\narea, though an upper level trough and incoming cold front will\nlead to increasing cloudiness and some cooler air working back \nin. By Saturday afternoon/evening, models start to diverge a \ndecent amount on the evolution of the trough and a potential \nsurface low over the Southern Plains. Most ensemble members spin\nup a decent low to our south as the trough and bring associated\nprecipitation on the north side across much of the forecast \narea. While rain is by far the favored precip type, there are a \ndecent number of solutions that give us snow, or at the very \nleast a mix of rain and snow. It'll depend how much cold air is \nable to work in ahead of the system. All that said, there are a \nhandful of solutions that show a pretty weak trough and quite a \nbit of dry air in place, giving us only some very light rain, or\neven keeping most of the area completely dry. So bottom line, \nforecast confidence Saturday afternoon into Sunday is rather \nlow, though the most likely outcome is that we see at least some\nlight rain move through the area with overall precipitation \nchances topping out in the 50-70% range. Behind that system, \nupper level and surface ridging quickly build back in, so high \ntemperatures look to remain in the mid 40s to lower 50s into \nearly next week.",
